Yes — and it's worth naming now what survives the jump, because two of the things this page leans on are cubic accidents.
What carries over unchanged. The step is just z = u + i·v with the three sides (u²−v², 2uv, u²+v²)·dt. Nothing there cares about degree. Sample at a rational midpoint and every side is still an exact rational triple, so the whole-number sides, the exact total length, and the "no square roots anywhere" property survive a quintic. The 1/N² drift survives too — midpoint is second order for any smooth velocity, so doubling the triangles still quarters the error.
What does not survive.
- y stops being exact. It is exact here only because y′ = 2t is linear and the midpoint rule is exact on a straight line. For a quintic, u and v are quadratic, so 2uv is quartic and the midpoint rule leaves a residue on the height as well as the sideways coordinate. The property that made the ends look tidy — height dead on, everything wrong at the far tip — disappears. Both coordinates will drift, in the same direction, at the same order.
- The grid gets much dearer. With a step width whose bottom number is q, evaluating a quadratic u and v at the midpoint puts q² underneath, squaring gives q⁴, and the step width adds one more, so the pitch goes roughly as q⁵ rather than q³. Dense quintic chains with whole-number sides will cost hundreds of times more cells than the cubic — or the chain has to accept non-integer sides. That is the number I'd want before committing to quintics anywhere.
- The lattice test hardens. Here it was one line: y = t² whole forces t whole, then 3 divides t. With degree-5 coordinates the same question becomes a degree-5 condition in t with no shortcut, and there is no reason to expect a tidy family like t = 0, 3, 6.
What I'd do cheaply now, before any quintic work. Make chain.py take u(t) and v(t) as inputs instead of hard-coding 1 and t, so the cubic is just the first case and a quadratic pair drops in without touching the tables. Twenty lines, no change to any number already on the page. The grid law should be derived per family rather than carried over — 4 × q³ is not a quintic fact.
The projection-level point also stands: the repo rule is cubic first, quintic only where no cubic chain meets G3, so quintics will be a handful of curves rather than a gallery — which suits the q⁵ cost, but means each one needs its own pitch analysis.
Say the word and I'll make the u, v refactor and write these degree-generalities into tph-tri-memory.md. I won't start any quintic tables until you ask.

