Pitch is cells per unit of the drawing — one unit across, one unit up. Not distance along the curve, and not a fraction of t. Three separate things get confused here:
- t is the knob that runs along the curve. It isn't a length. t = 3 just means the third unit of the knob.
- step width is a slice of t: the stopping point divided by the triangle count. Twelve triangles out to t = 3 gives 1/4.
- pitch is how many grid cells fit in one unit of the plane. This is what the rule computes, and it comes from the step width only — because the step's sides are (1 − t²)·dt and 2t·dt, so the denominators of dt decide everything.
Total grid = pitch × how many units the curve spans. The pitch alone never tells you the size of the drawing, because the span comes from the stopping point, not from the grid:
stopping point	curve spans, units	pitch	total grid, cells
3	12 × 9	1	12 × 9
3	12 × 9	256	3072 × 2304
12/5	552/125 × 144/25	500	2208 × 2880
24/5	8016/125 × 576/25	125	8016 × 2880
24/5	8016/125 × 576/25	3125	200400 × 72000
That table is now the first section of the sandbox, before anything else, because that's exactly where I was being unclear.
The punchline: 3125 is a pitch, not a total. If you want a round total, the stopping point has to make the span round — and t = 3 is the only stop here that does, spanning exactly 12 × 9 units. That's why the lattice glyph reads cleanly: it is a 12 × 9 grid at pitch 1, and the same drawing is 3072 × 2304 cells at the pitch the step width forces.
And to close the loop on "bigger than the curve": the lattice is unbounded; the numbers above are only how much of it the curve occupies. A 3125 pitch means one unit of the drawing is 3125 cells — the curve just fills 200400 × 72000 of them.

