The algebra. chain.py uses the PH form x = t − t³/3, y = t², i.e. 9x² = y(y−3)². Rotate it — (X, Y) = (y, x) — and you get 9Y² = X(X−3)², which is the repo's own alpha curve 18y² = x(x−6)² at half scale. Checked exactly at t = 1, 2, 3, 6/5: both sides agree every time. So the classical picture has the node at the origin, the loop opening right, arms to the right — horizontal. T1 stands it up.
Why it's vertical. x' = 1−t², y' = 2t is the standard PH hodograph (speed 1+t²), so (t − t³/3, t²) is the CAGD convention. Both orientations are "standard" in their own literature — but within this repo the alpha form in HANDOFF-int.md is horizontal, so T1 is the odd one out.
The cost of rotating. It isn't just the drawing: chain.py's integral/speed swap to X = t², Y = t³/3 − t; every sandbox table and the literal pairs in the prose follow; make_results.py's "the y coordinate is exact because the vertical speed is linear" becomes the x coordinate (x' = 2t is linear, y' = t²−1 is not); T1's domains, grid, label rows and PNG all get redone. The audit stays valid and re-derives from chain.py.

