The general rule — now a page panel, and five lines in the JSON as how_to_find:
1. Never let v be a multiple of u. v = k·u pins arg(w), so the curve is straight (bend 1.00); if that line passes the origin you get a cusp instead. Bend needs v/u to vary with t.
2. Choose the heading turn first. The heading is 2·arg(w), so the turn is twice the sweep of the speed path. Measured here: 167° → bend 2.00; 186° → 2.0–2.7; 215° → 5.58; past ~230° it loops.
3. Use the even pair u = u₀ − u₂t + u₂t², v = v₀ − v₂t + v₂t² — point symmetry puts the inflection at exactly t = ½ and shares the turn evenly.
4. Fine-tune with the closest approach — nearer the origin adds bend at the same turn, but keep it strictly above zero or the speed vanishes.
5. Clear the denominators last — the pitch is the lcm, and scaling the recipe by n divides the pitch by n²; decimals cost pitch.
6. Read three numbers before keeping it: bend, inflection inside (0,1), closest approach > 0. No inflection means a C; closest 0 means a cusp.
Worked example on the page: the even pair with u₂ = v₂ = 4, v₀ = 0.7 is bend 2.69 at pitch 200 → drop v₀ to 0.5 → bend 2.24 at pitch 120 → double the recipe → pitch 30.
One caveat on step 2: the turn predicts the bend well but not exactly — 186° yields both 2.00 and 2.69 depending on how the speed magnitude varies, so it is a guide, not a formula.

