arc   = integral of |w(h)|^2 dh   over [-1/2, 1/2]
chord = integral of  w(h)^2  dh   over [-1/2, 1/2]
bend  = arc / |chord|
For deg w = 2 this gives the weights 1, 1/12, 1/80. For deg w = 3 it gives more terms with new weights (1, 1/12, 1/80, 1/448, ... — computed, not remembered). Same code, different degree.
The pitch. Still the lcm of the integrated coefficients' denominators. It grows fast — degree 7 has more coefficients, so a decimal like 1.5 costs more. The "clear the denominators first" discipline matters more, not less.
The integer-hit method. The bounding-box gcd(x(t) - X, y(t) - Y) works at any degree; the polynomials are just longer. The pitch stops get less complete as the degree rises, so the gcd method becomes the only honest one.
