In numerical mathematics and geometry, the specific discretization method you are using is the midpoint rule (also called midpoint quadrature or midpoint tangent-hodograph discretization).

How the Discretization Works

To turn the smooth, continuous T-PH curve into a discrete chain of Pythagorean triangles, your method breaks the curve's velocity (hodograph) into uniform steps:
• Uniform parameter partitioning. You divide the total parameter interval \([0, t_{\max}]\) into \(N\) equal sub-intervals of width \(\Delta t = \frac{t_{\max}}{N}\).
• Midpoint velocity sampling. Instead of evaluating the velocity at the start or end of each step, you evaluate the generator polynomials \(u(t)\) and \(v(t)\) at the exact center (midpoint) of each interval: \(t_i = \left(i + \frac{1}{2}\right)\Delta t\).
• Exact rational integration. Multiplying the midpoint velocity \((x'(t_i), y'(t_i))\) and speed \(\sigma(t_i)\) by \(\Delta t\) produces a straight-line segment whose components \((\Delta x_i, \Delta y_i, \Delta s_i)\) form an exact rational Pythagorean triple.

Why This Specific Method Is Ideal Here

• Preserves the Pythagorean property. Evaluating \(u(t)\) and \(v(t)\) at rational midpoints guarantees that every discrete segment obeys \(\Delta x_i^2 + \Delta y_i^2 = \Delta s_i^2\) with zero square-root approximations.
• Second-order accuracy. Standard forward stepping (Euler's method) accumulates noticeable drift as the curve bends. Sampling at the midpoint cancels out first-order error, making the triangle chain converge four times faster as you double the number of steps.
• Exact \(y\)-coordinates. Because \(y'(t) = 2t\) is a linear function, midpoint sampling integrates \(y(t)\) with zero mathematical error regardless of how few triangles you use.
