In computer-aided geometric design (CAGD) and math literature, you can shorten Tschirnhausen cubic Pythagorean-hodograph curve to a few standard acronyms depending on your focus:

Common Abbreviations

• T-PH curve: The most direct shorthand used when specifically distinguishing the Tschirnhausen cubic from higher-degree PH curves.
• Cubic PH curve: Since the Tschirnhausen cubic is the only non-degenerate polynomial cubic that has the Pythagorean-hodograph property, calling it a "cubic PH curve" uniquely identifies it in geometric literature. but dont use here.
• PH cubic: An even shorter, widely understood variation of "cubic PH curve" used in CAD and CNC machining papers. but dont use here



approximate the curve with a chain of Pythagorean triangles, and  connect them hypotenuse-to-hypotenuse.
this geometric construction is the exact discrete analog of a Pythagorean-hodograph (PH) curve. When you trace a curve using discrete steps, each step forms a right triangle whose legs are the horizontal (\(\Delta x\)) and vertical (\(\Delta y\)) displacements, and whose hypotenuse is the straight-line chord length (\(\Delta s\)).

How the Triangle Chain Works

• The Pythagorean condition. For a smooth T-PH curve, the velocity components satisfy \(x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2 = \sigma(t)^2\) at every single point. In a discrete approximation, each segment satisfies \(\Delta x_i^2 + \Delta y_i^2 = \Delta s_i^2\), where \(\Delta x_i\), \(\Delta y_i\), and \(\Delta s_i\) are rational numbers (or scaled integer Pythagorean triples).
• Connecting the chain. You place the triangles tip-to-tail along the curve. The end of one hypotenuse becomes the starting vertex of the next triangle's legs, forming a continuous piecewise-linear path (a polyline) where every segment has an exact rational length.
• Generating the triangles. You can generate each triangle in the chain directly from the T-PH curve's underlying polynomials \(u(t)\) and \(v(t)\) by evaluating them at discrete parameter steps \(t_{i}\):
\(\Delta x_{i}\propto u(t_{i})^{2}-v(t_{i})^{2},\quad \Delta y_{i}\propto 2u(t_{i})v(t_{i}),\quad \Delta s_{i}\propto u(t_{i})^{2}+v(t_{i})^{2}\)
Because \(u(t)\) and \(v(t)\) are linear polynomials for a cubic PH curve, plugging in any rational values for \(t_{i}\) guarantees a valid Pythagorean triple.

Why This Is Useful

• Exact total arc length. Because every hypotenuse \(\Delta s_i\) is an exact rational number rather than an irrational square root, you can sum the lengths of the entire chain without any floating-point rounding errors.
• CNC and digital fabrication. Stepper motors and digital plotters move in discrete grid steps (\(\Delta x, \Delta y\)). Using Pythagorean triples lets a machine follow a smooth curve while keeping the true diagonal step distance exact at every pulse.

Key Trade-Off to Watch

• Angle alignment vs. rational steps. If you force the triangle legs to stay strictly aligned with the global \(x\) and \(y\) axes, the hypotenuses will naturally trace the curve's tangent directions. However, if you try to rotate each triangle so its legs align with the previous hypotenuse, the side lengths will lose their rational-number property unless the rotation angles themselves come from Pythagorean triples.
