Here is a worked example showing how a 4-triangle Pythagorean chain approximates a standard T-PH curve from \(t = 0\) to \(t = 2\).

1. Define the T-PH Curve

Using the simplest generator polynomials \(u(t) = 1\) and \(v(t) = t\), we get the exact velocity components and speed:
• Horizontal velocity: \(x'(t) = u(t)^2 - v(t)^2 = 1 - t^2\)
• Vertical velocity: \(y'(t) = 2u(t)v(t) = 2t\)
• Speed (hypotenuse rate): \(\sigma(t) = u(t)^2 + v(t)^2 = 1 + t^2\)
Integrating these from \(t = 0\) gives the smooth curve equations \(x(t) = t - \frac{t^3}{3}\) and \(y(t) = t^2\), with exact arc length \(s(t) = t + \frac{t^3}{3}\).

2. Generate the 4 Pythagorean Triangles

To build a 4-segment chain over \(t \in [0, 2]\), we divide the interval into 4 steps of width \(\Delta t = 0.5\) and evaluate the formulas at each step's midpoint (\(t = 0.25, 0.75, 1.25, 1.75\)). Multiplying the velocities by \(\Delta t = 0.5\) yields the exact dimensions of each right triangle:

