Using a base-5 grid like \(3125\) (\(5^{5}\)) with \(5n\) divisions works great because powers of 5 give clean, terminating decimals and exact integer grid steps.
In fact, if you choose \(N = 25\) (\(n = 5\)) over \(t \in [0, 2]\), every single triangle leg and hypotenuse lands on exact integer coordinates when scaled to a 3125-unit grid.

1. Exact Integer Grid Scaling (\(3125 = 5^5\))

For \(N = 5n\) equal steps over \(t \in [0, 2]\), the step width is \(\Delta t = \frac{2}{5n}\), and the midpoint of step \(i\) (from \(0\) to \(N-1\)) is \(t_i = \frac{2i + 1}{5n}\).
Plugging this into the T-PH formulas gives exact rational steps:
• Horizontal leg: \(\Delta x_i = \frac{2\left((5n)^2 - (2i + 1)^2\right)}{125n^3}\)
• Vertical leg: \(\Delta y_i = \frac{4(2i + 1)}{25n^2}\)
• Hypotenuse: \(\Delta s_i = \frac{2\left((5n)^2 + (2i + 1)^2\right)}{125n^3}\)
Why \(3125\) is a magic grid size: When \(n = 5\) (\(N = 25\) triangles), the denominator \(125n^{3}\) equals \(15,625\), and every numerator is divisible by \(5\). Multiplying everything by \(3125\) turns every triangle into exact integers with zero rounding error!
